## Example Outlines

EXAMPLE 1 — Short presentation (8 cards):

<section imageLayout="behind"><instructions>Title card with dramatic jungle backdrop feel. Set the tone for wonder and surprise.</instructions><h1>Fabulous Frogs: Nature's Most Surprising Creatures</h1></section>
<section imageLayout="right"><instructions>Vivid close-up composition — the frog's bright color is the star. Emphasize danger vs beauty contrast.</instructions><h1>The Golden Poison Dart Frog</h1><ul><li>One frog contains enough toxin to kill 10 adult humans</li><li>Indigenous Emberá people use it to poison hunting darts</li></ul></section>
<section imageLayout="left"><instructions>Translucent macro photography feel. The visual of visible organs should be the centerpiece.</instructions><h1>The Glass Frog's Invisible Trick</h1><ul><li>Transparent skin reveals beating heart and visible organs</li><li>Watch eggs develop in real-time through their belly</li></ul></section>
<section imageLayout="blank" group="comparison"><instructions>Side-by-side comparison layout — contrast the extremes. Use bold labels to anchor each data point.</instructions><h1>Size Extremes</h1><ul><li><b>Paedophryne amauensis:</b> 7.7mm — world's smallest vertebrate (Papua New Guinea)</li><li><b>Goliath Frog:</b> 32cm, 3.3kg — size of a house cat (Cameroon)</li></ul></section>
<section imageLayout="right"><instructions>Frozen landscape mood. Build narrative tension — death, then miraculous revival.</instructions><h1>The Wood Frog's Death-Defying Freeze</h1><ul><li>Survives being frozen solid for months</li><li>Heart stops, blood freezes, brain activity ceases</li><li>Thaws in spring and hops away unharmed</li></ul></section>
