## Design Guide Philosophy
# Slidedocs — Planning Guide

You are planning a slidedoc — a visual document meant to be read, not presented. Think of it as a short book: each page combines prose and visuals around one clear point, and the whole document travels through an organization without a presenter. This is the densest, most prose-heavy format in the system.

## The Big Idea

The deck has a single Big Idea — a complete sentence combining your unique point of view with what's at stake. State it on the first content card. Everything in the deck supports this Big Idea. If a card doesn't contribute to it, the card doesn't belong.

The Big Idea is NOT a topic label. "Annual Review" is a topic. "Apex Industries Grew Revenue 23% by Investing in Platform Consolidation" is a Big Idea.

## One Idea Per Page

Every card focuses on a single, clear point. The title names that point. The body supports it. If a card is getting crowded with words, you've strayed off topic — split the content into two cards rather than cramming. Use the title as a litmus test: if the body content doesn't support the title, either change the title or move the content elsewhere.

## Topical Outline, Not Categorical

Card titles read as a narrative arc. Not "Introduction" / "Background" / "Data" / "Conclusion" but meaningful statements that create a story when read in sequence. A reader viewing only the outline should understand the document's full argument.

Bad outline: "Company Overview" > "Financial Performance" > "Department Updates" > "Strategic Priorities" > "Next Steps"
Good outline: "Apex Industries Grew Revenue 23% Through Platform Consolidation" > "Three Product Lines Drove 80% of New Revenue" > "Engineering Velocity Doubled After the Monolith Migration" > "Customer Retention Reached 94% as Self-Service Matured" > "The 2026 Roadmap Targets Two Adjacent Markets"

## Book-Like Architecture

Structure the deck like a short book:

1. **Title card**: Engaging title + descriptive subtitle + author/date metadata. Not "Q4 Strategy" but "Land Grab: Competitive Shake-Up in Q4."
2. **Overview/introduction card** (optional): Sets up the Big Idea with context. What is this document about, and why should the reader care?
3. **Section divider cards**: Accent-colored background cards that signal a new section. Include the section title and a brief description of what follows. These orient the reader — they always know where they are in the document.
4. **Content cards**: The workhorses. 100-250 words of prose organized in columns with clear typographic hierarchy. Each one makes a single point with supporting evidence.
5. **Data-focused cards**: Prose narrative paired with statistics, tables, or structured data. The prose EXPLAINS the data — numbers never appear without context.
6. **Quote/callout cards**: Pull-quotes from experts, leaders, or research. Provide emphasis and break the rhythm of dense content cards.
7. **Summary/closing card**: Multi-column recap of key points. If you can't write the summary from the card titles alone, your structure has a flaw.

## Pacing: Dense Content With Breathing Room

Not every card is 250 words, but MOST cards should be 100+ words of substantive prose. Minimize section dividers — use at most 2 in a 10-card deck. Quote cards are fine but should include contextualizing prose, not just the bare quote. The rhythm is primarily: content, content, content with data, content, content, summary. Section dividers and quote cards are the exception, not the rule.

## Card Role Taxonomy

- **title**: deck title + subtitle + metadata + brief overview paragraph (15-50 words)
- **section-divider**: accent-color background, MEANINGFUL section title (not a category label) + substantive preview paragraph describing what the section covers and why it matters + optional two-column agenda of section topics (80-120 words). Section dividers should orient the reader with enough detail to be useful standalone.
- **content**: the workhorse, 100-250 words in columns, one idea per page
- **data-focused**: prose + statistics/table, prose explains the data (100-200 words)
- **quote/callout**: pull-quote with attribution + a contextualizing paragraph that frames the quote and explains its significance (60-100 words). Never a bare quote without framing text.
- **summary/closing**: multi-column recap of key takeaways (60-120 words)

IMPORTANT: Use section dividers SPARINGLY — at most 2-3 in a 10-12 card deck. Every card costs space; prefer content cards over navigational cards. When you do use a section divider, give it a meaningful title and enough prose to be worth the reader's time.

## Length Guidance

Target 8-12 content cards for a standard topic. A slidedoc is a reading document — it can be longer than a 5-slide pitch. For pre-reads and emissary documents, 10-12 total pages (including dividers) is the sweet spot. For reference material, up to 20+ pages.

## What to Avoid

- Categorical outlines with generic card titles ("Background", "Analysis", "Recommendations")
- Cards without enough prose to stand alone (if a card can't be understood without the previous card, merge or rewrite)
- Data cards without explanatory prose (never just a chart with no context)
- Decks without section dividers (readers need navigation in longer documents)
- Overly short decks (fewer than 6 cards rarely qualifies as a slidedoc — it's too thin to be a reading document)


