## GML Examples
# Slidedocs — GML Examples

Reference cards showing how slidedoc layouts translate to GML. Each example demonstrates dense prose, multi-column structure, and document-like readability.

## Title card (conceptual title + descriptive subtitle + metadata)

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank">
  <DIV class="body">
    <H1>Apex Industries 2025 Annual Review: Platform Consolidation Drove 23% Revenue Growth and Record Customer Retention</H1>
    <P><I>A comprehensive review of financial performance, operational milestones, and strategic priorities for the year ahead</I></P>
    <P>Prepared by the Office of the CEO | January 2026</P>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Content card — two-column prose (the workhorse)

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank">
  <DIV class="body">
    <LABELS><LABEL variant="solid">PERFORMANCE</LABEL></LABELS>
    <H1>Three Product Lines Drove 80% of New Revenue While Legacy Services Declined Gracefully</H1>
    <P><I>The shift from project-based consulting to recurring platform revenue reached a tipping point in Q3, with subscription revenue surpassing services for the first time in company history.</I></P>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[50,50]">
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Platform Revenue Accelerated</B></H4>
        <P>The analytics platform grew 47% year-over-year, reaching $142M in annual recurring revenue. This acceleration was driven by three factors: the launch of the self-service tier in March, which expanded the addressable market to mid-size companies; the enterprise API partnership with Salesforce, which generated $28M in co-sold deals; and the introduction of usage-based pricing, which increased average contract values by 31% for existing customers.</P>
        <P>Customer acquisition costs fell 34% as the self-service funnel matured, dropping from $12,400 per enterprise customer to $8,200. The payback period shortened from 14 months to 9 months.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Legacy Services Managed Down</B></H4>
        <P>Professional services revenue declined 18% to $67M as planned. The strategic decision to sunset fixed-scope implementation projects freed 120 consultants to transition into customer success roles supporting the platform business. This reallocation improved gross margin by 8 percentage points while maintaining a 94% customer satisfaction score during the transition.</P>
        <P>The remaining services engagements are exclusively platform implementation and integration work, which carries 52% margins compared to 28% for the legacy consulting model. Services now function as a platform adoption accelerator rather than a standalone revenue line.</P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Content card — three-column comparison

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank">
  <DIV class="body">
    <LABELS><LABEL variant="solid">STRATEGY</LABEL></LABELS>
    <H1>The 2026 Roadmap Targets Three Adjacent Markets With Distinct Entry Strategies</H1>
    <P><I>Each market requires a different go-to-market approach based on competitive dynamics, buyer maturity, and existing Apex customer overlap.</I></P>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[33,33,34]">
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Healthcare Analytics</B></H4>
        <P>The $4.2B healthcare analytics market is dominated by legacy vendors with aging platforms. Apex's competitive advantage is real-time data processing, which existing players cannot match without rebuilding their architectures. Entry strategy: acquire MedInsight (valuation $180M, 340 hospital customers) and cross-sell Apex's platform to their installed base within 18 months.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Financial Compliance</B></H4>
        <P>Regulatory technology spending grew 28% in 2025 as new SEC reporting requirements expanded the compliance burden for mid-market firms. Apex's existing data pipeline integrations position it well for automated compliance reporting. Entry strategy: build a compliance module on the existing platform and launch with 12 design partners from the current customer base by Q2 2026.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Supply Chain Intelligence</B></H4>
        <P>Post-pandemic supply chain visibility remains the top operational priority for manufacturing executives. The $6.8B market is fragmented with no dominant platform. Entry strategy: partner with three logistics providers to embed Apex analytics into their existing workflows, reaching 2,000+ manufacturers through channel distribution rather than direct sales.</P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Data card — prose narrative + stats

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank">
  <DIV class="body">
    <LABELS><LABEL variant="solid">FINDINGS</LABEL></LABELS>
    <H1>Customer Retention Reached 94% as Self-Service Capabilities Eliminated the Renewal Friction Point</H1>
    <P><I>The Q1 customer research study identified self-service as the strongest predictor of renewal — customers who adopted the self-service portal renewed at 97% compared to 82% for those who relied on managed service.</I></P>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[50,50]">
      <DIV>
        <P>The research team surveyed 1,240 customers across four segments: enterprise (500+ employees), mid-market (100-499), growth (25-99), and startup (under 25). The most striking finding was the correlation between self-service adoption depth and renewal probability. Customers who completed three or more self-service workflows per month had a 97% renewal rate, compared to 82% for customers who relied primarily on account managers for configuration changes.</P>
        <P>This finding has significant implications for the 2026 product roadmap. Investing in self-service capabilities is not just a cost reduction play — it directly drives retention. The product team has reprioritized the self-service builder to ship in Q2 rather than Q4.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <SMART-LAYOUT variant="stats" cellSize="15">
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="94%">
            <P>Overall customer retention rate, up from 83% in 2024</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="97%">
            <P>Renewal rate for customers with 3+ self-service workflows per month</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="1,240">
            <P>Customers surveyed across four segments in the Q1 research study</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="34%">
            <P>Reduction in support tickets after self-service portal launch</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
        </SMART-LAYOUT>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Quote/callout card (accent-color background + pull-quote)

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank" background-color="#1E3A5F">
  <DIV class="body">
    <BLOCKQUOTE><P><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF"><I>"The shift from quarterly board presentations to monthly slidedoc updates changed how our leadership team makes decisions. Instead of debating data in the room, everyone arrives prepared. The conversation starts at the strategic level because the analytical groundwork has already been read and absorbed."</I></SPAN></P></BLOCKQUOTE>
    <P><B><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">Sarah Chen, Chief Strategy Officer</SPAN></B></P>
    <P><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">Apex Industries | Board Strategy Review, October 2025</SPAN></P>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Section divider (accent-color background + section title)

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank" background-color="#2563EB">
  <DIV class="body">
    <H1><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">Financial Performance and Revenue Analysis</SPAN></H1>
    <P><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">This section examines revenue growth across product lines, margin expansion from the services-to-platform transition, and the financial impact of the self-service pricing model introduced in Q1.</SPAN></P>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Summary card — multi-column recap

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank">
  <DIV class="body">
    <LABELS><LABEL variant="solid">SUMMARY</LABEL></LABELS>
    <H1>2025 Proved the Platform Thesis — 2026 Must Execute on Adjacent Market Expansion</H1>
    <P><I>The key findings from this annual review support accelerating investment in the three growth initiatives identified in the strategic roadmap.</I></P>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[33,33,34]">
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>What Worked</B></H4>
        <P>Platform revenue surpassed services for the first time, reaching $142M ARR with 47% growth. Self-service capabilities drove retention to 94%. Customer acquisition costs fell 34% as the funnel matured. The services-to-platform transition improved gross margins by 8 points.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>What Needs Attention</B></H4>
        <P>Enterprise deal cycles lengthened from 6 to 9 months as procurement teams added AI governance reviews. The EMEA expansion underperformed targets by 22%, primarily due to data residency concerns that the Q3 infrastructure update will address. Engineering hiring fell 15% short of plan.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>2026 Priorities</B></H4>
        <P>Execute on three adjacent market entries: healthcare analytics (acquisition), financial compliance (build), and supply chain intelligence (partner). Target $200M ARR by year-end. Achieve SOC 2 Type II and HIPAA compliance by Q2 to unlock healthcare and financial verticals. Hire 80 engineers.</P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

## Content card with table — prose + comparison data

```html
<SECTION image-layout="blank">
  <DIV class="body">
    <LABELS><LABEL variant="solid">ANALYSIS</LABEL></LABELS>
    <H1>Engineering Velocity Doubled After the Monolith Migration but Incident Rates Need Further Reduction</H1>
    <P><I>The migration from the legacy monolithic architecture to microservices completed in August, and the productivity gains exceeded initial projections across every team metric.</I></P>
    <P>The engineering organization completed 2,847 pull requests in Q4, compared to 1,390 in Q4 2024. Deployment frequency increased from twice weekly to continuous delivery, with an average of 12 production deployments per day. These gains reflect not just architectural improvements but also the maturation of the CI/CD pipeline and the adoption of feature flags that decouple deployment from release.</P>
    <TABLE>
      <TR><TH>Metric</TH><TH>Q4 2024 (Monolith)</TH><TH>Q4 2025 (Microservices)</TH><TH>Change</TH></TR>
      <TR><TD>Pull requests merged</TD><TD>1,390</TD><TD>2,847</TD><TD>+105%</TD></TR>
      <TR><TD>Deployment frequency</TD><TD>2x per week</TD><TD>12x per day</TD><TD>+42x</TD></TR>
      <TR><TD>Mean time to recovery</TD><TD>4.2 hours</TD><TD>23 minutes</TD><TD>-91%</TD></TR>
      <TR><TD>P1 incidents per month</TD><TD>3.1</TD><TD>1.8</TD><TD>-42%</TD></TR>
      <TR><TD>Developer satisfaction (eNPS)</TD><TD>+12</TD><TD>+47</TD><TD>+35 pts</TD></TR>
    </TABLE>
    <ASIDE variant="note">
      <P>While incident rates dropped 42%, the target for 2026 is fewer than 1.0 P1 incidents per month. The observability team is investing in predictive alerting to catch degradations before they become customer-facing incidents.</P>
    </ASIDE>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
```

