    <p>Lithium-ion battery costs crashed from $1,220/kWh in 2010 to $139/kWh in 2024, with utility-scale installations achieving $89/kWh in competitive markets. This 89% cost decline accelerated dramatically in the past 24 months as Chinese manufacturing capacity expanded 340% and new cathode chemistries improved energy density.</p>
    <p><b>Manufacturing scale effects</b> drove the steepest cost reductions, with CATL and BYD achieving gigawatt-hour production volumes that enabled automated manufacturing processes previously impossible at smaller scales. Tesla's 4680 cell technology contributed additional cost reductions through simplified manufacturing and improved thermal management.</p>
    <p><b>Grid integration demand</b> tripled from 6.2 GW in 2022 to 18.7 GW in 2024 as utilities deployed storage to manage renewable energy intermittency. California led deployment with 5.2 GW of new capacity, followed by Texas (3.1 GW) and New York (1.8 GW). Grid-scale storage now provides 12% of peak demand capacity in California during summer months.</p>
    <p>Integration challenges persist around grid synchronization and frequency regulation, with utilities reporting 15-20% higher operational complexity when storage exceeds 30% of grid capacity. Advanced inverter technology and AI-driven grid management systems address these challenges but require significant utility infrastructure investment.</p>

