    <H1>Customer Retention Reached 94% as Self-Service Capabilities Eliminated the Renewal Friction Point</H1>
    <P><I>The Q1 customer research study identified self-service as the strongest predictor of renewal — customers who adopted the self-service portal renewed at 97% compared to 82% for those who relied on managed service.</I></P>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[50,50]">
      <DIV>
        <P>The research team surveyed 1,240 customers across four segments: enterprise (500+ employees), mid-market (100-499), growth (25-99), and startup (under 25). The most striking finding was the correlation between self-service adoption depth and renewal probability. Customers who completed three or more self-service workflows per month had a 97% renewal rate, compared to 82% for customers who relied primarily on account managers for configuration changes.</P>
        <P>This finding has significant implications for the 2026 product roadmap. Investing in self-service capabilities is not just a cost reduction play — it directly drives retention. The product team has reprioritized the self-service builder to ship in Q2 rather than Q4.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <SMART-LAYOUT variant="stats" cellSize="15">
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="94%">
            <P>Overall customer retention rate, up from 83% in 2024</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="97%">
            <P>Renewal rate for customers with 3+ self-service workflows per month</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="1,240">
            <P>Customers surveyed across four segments in the Q1 research study</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
          <SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM number="34%">
            <P>Reduction in support tickets after self-service portal launch</P>
          </SMART-LAYOUT-ITEM>
        </SMART-LAYOUT>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>
