# Slidedocs — Structure Guide

You are deciding the spatial composition of each card. Slidedoc cards are document pages — dense prose organized in multi-column grids with clear typographic hierarchy. Your job: arrange text, data, and emphasis elements into a readable page layout.

## Core Principles

**Multi-column grid is the default.** 2-column and 3-column layouts are the workhorses. Content fills columns with prose. A single column of text spanning full width is too document-like — break it into columns for scannability. Set column widths so lines are 50-70 characters (12-15 words) for optimal reading.

**Typographic hierarchy on every card.** Every content card uses a three-tier type system:
1. Large heading (6-10 words): the card's main point
2. Thesis subhead: a complete sentence in italic accent-color text summarizing the takeaway, placed directly below the heading
3. Body paragraphs in columns: the substance, organized into scannable text blocks

**White space for readability, not drama.** Generous margins, clear gutters between columns. But space serves structure — every zone is filled with purpose. This is not a sparse design aesthetic; it's a dense document that uses space to organize, not to impress.

**Figure-to-text proximity.** Data, diagrams, and statistics must appear on the same card as the prose that references them. Never "see next page." Words and visuals are co-located.

## Content Types

Use these terms in your spatial descriptions:

- **prose column** — a column of flowing paragraphs with optional sub-headers and bold emphasis. The primary content type in a slidedoc.
- **thesis subhead** — an italic accent-colored sentence below the heading that summarizes the card's key point. Signature slidedoc element.
- **pull-quote** — a large italic statement that breaks the column grid for emphasis. Used frequently (every 3-4 cards) to highlight key insights.
- **data panel** — statistics, metrics, or structured data co-located with explanatory prose.
- **comparison columns** — three equal columns comparing options, approaches, or categories. Each column has a header + 2-3 sentences of prose.
- **narrative block** — a dense paragraph (3-5 sentences) of explanatory text.
- **section marker** — an accent-colored label identifying the section context.
- **summary grid** — multi-column recap of key points, used on closing cards.

## Composition Patterns

Use at least 4 different patterns across the deck. Slidedocs need visual variety to sustain a longer reading experience.

### Title card (card 0 only)
Conceptual, engaging title in large text. Descriptive subtitle below. Author name and date as metadata. Light background preferred. This is the book cover — it should make the reader want to dive in. No dense prose needed, but the title must be specific and meaningful.

### Two-column prose (primary workhorse)
Heading at top. Italic thesis subhead below the heading. Two equal columns of flowing prose below, each with its own bold sub-header. This is the bread-and-butter slidedoc page — dense, readable, and structured. 150+ words total. Both columns contain real content.

### Three-column comparison
Heading + thesis subhead at top. Three equal columns below, each with a bold header and 2-3 sentences of explanatory prose. Used for comparing approaches, options, benefit categories, or process phases. Every column has substance — no single-word entries.

### Prose + data panel
Two columns. One column: flowing prose narrative explaining findings or context. Other column: statistics, metrics, a table, or structured data. The prose column EXPLAINS the data column — they work as a unit. The data is never unexplained.

### Quote/callout card
Accent-color background. A large italic pull-quote with attribution. PLUS a contextualizing paragraph that frames the quote: who said it, why it matters, what insight the reader should take from it. Never a bare quote alone. 60-100 words total. Two-column layout preferred: quote in one column, context in the other.

### Section divider
Full accent-color background. Section title in large white text — the title must be MEANINGFUL, not a category label. "Platform Revenue Surpassed Services for the First Time" not "Financial Performance." Below the title: a substantive preview (2-3 sentences) describing what the section covers and why. Optionally, a two-column layout listing the section's key topics. 80-120 words. These must be useful standalone, not just decorative transitions.

### Summary card
Multi-column layout (2 or 3 columns) recapping key points from the document. Each column covers a different theme or section. Dense but organized — the reader should be able to review the entire document's argument by reading this one page.

### Content card with table
Heading + thesis subhead at top. Prose paragraph providing context. A comparison table below with clear headers, 3-5 rows of data. The table is always accompanied by explanatory text — never standalone.

## Layout Rules

CRITICAL: EVERY content card MUST use a multi-column layout (COLUMNS). No exceptions. The defining visual signature of a slidedoc is text organized into 2-3 columns, not a single centered text block.

- Content cards: ALWAYS use 2 columns (50/50) or 3 columns (33/33/34). Never a single column of text.
- Section dividers: use 2-column layout — title + preview paragraph in left column, topic list in right column
- Quote cards: use 2-column layout — pull-quote in one column, contextualizing prose in the other
- Title cards: can be single column but should include a brief overview paragraph
- Even column splits only — no asymmetric 40/60 or 30/70
- Section dividers used SPARINGLY — at most 2 in a 10-card deck
- Data always appears alongside explanatory prose on the same card
- The heading + thesis subhead + COLUMNS structure is the standard for every content card
- Light/white backgrounds for content pages; accent color backgrounds for dividers only

## What to Avoid

- Full-width single-column text blocks (too document-like, not scannable)
- Cards with only one content zone (too sparse for a slidedoc)
- Large decorative images (slidedocs use functional visuals, not decoration)
- Data without explanatory prose on the same card
- Sparse cards that look like projected presentation slides
- Cards without clear typographic hierarchy (heading + subhead + body)
- Centered text for prose paragraphs (left-aligned for readability)


